\date{2024-08-01}
\taxon{Definition}
\import{common}
\tag{groupoidal}
\title{Diagrammatic adjunctions}
\author{fredrikbakke}

\p{
  Given a functor #{R : \Fun(A,B)}, a (diagrammatic) \concept{left adjoint} to #{R} is a functor #{L : \Fun(B,A)} equipped with an element
  ##{
    \begin{aligned}
      \operatorname{\texttt{is-diagrammatic-left-adjoint}}(R,L)\defeq \\ \sigma-type{\eta \colon \id \Rightarrow R \o L}
   & \left(\sigma-type{\varepsilon \colon L \o R \Rightarrow \id}{\left(\varepsilon L \o L \eta = \id\right)}\right)
    \times \\
   & \left(\sigma-type{\varepsilon' \colon L \o R \Rightarrow \id}{\left(R\varepsilon' \o \eta R = \id\right)}\right)\mathrlap{,}
  \end{aligned}}

  or [equivalently](hott-000U),

    ##{
    \begin{aligned}
      \operatorname{\texttt{is-diagrammatic-left-adjoint}}'(R,L)\defeq \\ \sigma-type{\varepsilon \colon L \o R \Rightarrow \id}
   & \left(\sigma-type{\eta \colon \id \Rightarrow R \o L}{\left(\varepsilon L \o L \eta = \id\right)}\right)
    \times \\
   & \left(\sigma-type{\eta' \colon \id \Rightarrow R \o L}{\left(R\varepsilon \o \eta' R = \id\right)}\right)\mathrlap{.}
  \end{aligned}}
}

\p{
We must ask for two a priori unrelated counits or units for reasons of homotopy coherence. The statements above can be shown to be propositions when #{A} and #{B} are Segal, while the corresponding types with a single unit–counit pair would not be.}
